The extension sector, which can be defined as the network of services, programs, and institutions that facilitate the flow of agricultural knowledge and technologies to farmers and rural communities, serving as an intermediary between the sources of agricultural knowledge production and its users, has the potential to play a pivotal role in enhancing public awareness and mitigating the extent of product losses. Consequently, to foster sustainability within the agricultural sector and curtail resource wastage, it is imperative to first identify the factors contributing to losses at various stages. Following a thorough assessment of the current situation, it is essential to implement loss management strategies in the agricultural sector with foresight, taking into account evolving conditions to achieve sustainable development. This includes the establishment and enhancement of agricultural processing industries, the implementation of effective warehousing solutions, the development of advanced distribution networks, and the creation of suitable markets for agricultural products, all aimed at managing losses during the post-harvest stages. Agricultural waste and residues, both in developing and developed countries, constitute a huge environmental burden and untapped-into economic potential While developed countries typically have established value chains for a large part of agricultural waste and side streams, not only developing nations can improve, as the “billion ton report” from the USA, originating in 2016, demonstrates [196]. When mismanaged, agro-waste has detrimental impacts on soil, water, and air quality. These byproducts are an often unavoidable consequence of the widespread cultivation and utilization of agricultural products made for feed, food and materials. Inadequate management of agricultural waste and residues, including improper disposal methods, and inefficiencies along the entire value chain can lead to a multitude of losses, risks and threats. Organic fertilizer, bioenergy in the form of biogas and synthesis gas and biochar are just a few examples of upcycling agro-waste streams, with protein, fuels and materials being other value-added potential products. Carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus-cycles can be closed by applying circular concepts to agricultural production. 2nd generation biorefineries approach the integration level of petrochemical refineries, where multiple products are obtained from multiple feedstocks. Farmer training and innovation are equally important to improve agricultural operations from a waste-management perspective. Agricultural waste management is a critical issue that demands innovative solutions to ensure sustainable development and environmental conservation. This paper reviews various strategies for managing agricultural waste, including its utilization in livestock feeding, bioenergy production, composting, and mulching. By incorporating agricultural waste as an alternative ingredient in livestock feed formulations, we can decrease reliance on staple food commodities such as corn, wheat, and soy, thereby mitigating competition between human and animal consumption. Additionally, transforming agricultural residues into biofuels like biogas and synthesis gas not only provides renewable energy sources but also reduces greenhouse gas emissions. Implementing efficient composting and mulching techniques further enhances soil fertility and water retention, contributing to more productive and sustainable agricultural practices. The integration of these approaches represents a significant step towards achieving the goals of sustainable development and reducing environmental degradation. To address the identified knowledge gaps in agricultural waste management, future research should focus on several critical areas. Firstly, there is a need for advanced studies on the optimization of technologies such as precision agriculture and automated harvesting systems to minimize post-harvest losses and enhance efficiency. Secondly, research into sustainable packaging solutions, particularly biodegradable and edible packaging derived from agricultural waste, could significantly reduce environmental impact. Thirdly, further exploration of upcycling agricultural by-products into high-value products like bioplastics,


Waste 2025, 3, 18 25 of 32
natural dyes, and fibers is essential for creating new markets and reducing waste. Additionally, investigations into alternative protein sources, including single-cell proteins, insects, seaweed, duckweed, cultured meat, and leaf protein concentrate, could help meet global food demands while alleviating pressure on traditional resources. Fourthly, more comprehensive studies are required to evaluate the economic viability and scalability of bioenergy production from agricultural residues, focusing on second-generation biofuels and syngas. Lastly, enhancing understanding of the role of biochar in carbon sequestration and soil health, as well as its potential applications in environmental remediation, would contribute to sustainable practices. By addressing these research directions, we can bridge existing knowledge gaps and promote environmentally friendly and economically viable solutions for agricultural waste management. Nature does not know waste; Let us take this as example for agricultural practice.
